1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,560
Good morning everyone.

2
00:00:01,560 --> 00:00:05,020
Last week we were looking at the hunter-gatherers in Ireland

3
00:00:05,020 --> 00:00:07,150
across the Irish Sea from England.

4
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:13,335
Today, we're going to move on to the period between four and six thousand years ago,

5
00:00:13,335 --> 00:00:15,380
known as the Neolithic period

6
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:19,520
which is when a total farming economy was introduced in Ireland.

7
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:25,984
Now, there are several hypotheses about the origins of the first Neolithic settlers in Ireland,

8
00:00:25,984 --> 00:00:28,570
but most of these contain problems.

9
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:35,340
For instance, there are considerable archaeological difficulties about the theory that they came from England.

10
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:37,930
The evidence doesn't really add up.

11
00:00:37,930 --> 00:00:44,680
But there are even greater practical problems about the theory that they came directly from continental Europe.

12
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:49,730
For one thing, it's not clear just how sufficient numbers of men

13
00:00:49,730 --> 00:00:55,200
and women could have been transported to Ireland to establish a viable population.

14
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:58,760
As you know, the hunter-gatherer economy

15
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:03,150
which existed beforehand was based on small scattered groups.

16
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:10,023
The farming economy would almost certainly have required much larger communities to do all the work needed to plant

17
00:01:10,023 --> 00:01:13,640
and tend sufficient crops to sustain them through the year.

18
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:19,330
The early farmers kept various animals, including cattle and sheep.

19
00:01:19,330 --> 00:01:21,105
There's also evidence of pigs,

20
00:01:21,105 --> 00:01:26,430
but it is possible that these could have been descended from the native wild species.

21
00:01:26,430 --> 00:01:29,760
Now, we know from modern farming that

22
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:34,159
if the level of breeding stock falls below about three hundred females,

23
00:01:34,159 --> 00:01:37,820
the future of the species locally is at risk.

24
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:41,900
So we must assume that from the beginnings of Neolithic farming

25
00:01:42,520 --> 00:01:45,934
the number of breeding sheep would have considerably exceeded three hundred,

26
00:01:45,934 --> 00:01:50,360
and the national cattle herd must have been of a similar size.

27
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:55,530
The question is how these were brought to the area and where they came from.

28
00:01:55,530 --> 00:02:02,350
It's usually suggested that the Neolithic settlers used skin-covered boats to transport livestock.

29
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:07,290
But this method would have severely restricted the range of the colonizing fleets.

30
00:02:07,290 --> 00:02:16,270
The sheer volume of animal transport necessary means it's unlikely that this livestock could have been brought from anywhere further than England.

31
00:02:16,270 --> 00:02:24,015
What about crops? Well, two main cereal crops were introduced to Ireland during this time:

32
00:02:24,015 --> 00:02:27,630
wheat and barley, both in several varieties.

33
00:02:27,630 --> 00:02:32,140
The main evidence for their presence consists of impressions on pottery,

34
00:02:32,140 --> 00:02:39,110
where a cereal grain accidentally became embedded in the surface of a pot before it was fired.

35
00:02:39,110 --> 00:02:41,940
The grain itself was destroyed by the firing,

36
00:02:41,940 --> 00:02:44,500
but it left an impression on the pot

37
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:47,600
which could be studied and identified by botanists.

38
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:52,200
Let's turn our attention now to the farming technology available at that time.

39
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:54,556
Before the cereal crops could be planted,

40
00:02:54,556 --> 00:02:57,760
it would have been necessary to clear the forest

41
00:02:57,760 --> 00:02:59,940
and to break the ground by ploughing.

42
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:06,870
The stone blade of a plough has been discovered during excavation in County Mayo in western Ireland.

43
00:03:06,870 --> 00:03:12,290
The body of the plough would have been of wood and could have been drawn by people,

44
00:03:12,290 --> 00:03:14,840
but it's also likely that cattle were used.

45
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:16,990
Now, the cultivation of crops

46
00:03:16,990 --> 00:03:22,560
and the husbandry of livestock brought about changes in people's lifestyle

47
00:03:22,940 --> 00:03:25,160
such as the type of shelters they made.

48
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:31,400
For one thing, instead of moving from place to place they needed permanent dwellings.

49
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:40,250
The stone axes used to chop down trees to make these dwellings were far superior to any that the Stone Age hunter-gatherers used.

50
00:03:40,250 --> 00:03:47,420
To make the axes, sources of suitable stone had to be found and systematically exploited.

51
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:51,781
These so-called 'axe factories' were really quarries rather than factories,

52
00:03:51,781 --> 00:03:57,450
as the manufacture of the axes wasn't regularly performed on the quarry site.

53
00:03:57,450 --> 00:04:01,240
However, after the axe had been chipped into shape

54
00:04:01,340 --> 00:04:04,620
they needed water and sand for grinding and polishing,

55
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:09,400
so a high mountainside wouldn't have been an appropriate place for this.

56
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:16,115
So this final stage of the manufacture must have been carried out close to water and sure enough,

57
00:04:16,115 --> 00:04:19,100
there's ample evidence of this at coastal sites.

58
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:23,905
Now it's clear that these Neolithic axes were transported all over Ireland,

59
00:04:23,905 --> 00:04:27,910
as well as to Scotland and the south of England.

60
00:04:27,910 --> 00:04:34,640
It's not really surprising that axes from 'axe factories' in England have also been found in Ireland.

61
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:40,490
At the very least, this indicates that there was a link between the two islands during that period.

62
00:04:40,490 --> 00:04:45,562
One of the most useful innovations of the colonizers was pottery making,

63
00:04:45,562 --> 00:04:48,520
which was quite unknown to Irish hunter-gatherers.

64
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:52,978
The pottery was probably made by shaping clay into a ball with the hand,

65
00:04:52,978 --> 00:04:56,480
and then hollowing it until the walls were the right thickness.

66
00:04:56,480 --> 00:05:00,090
After firing, the outside was often polished.

67
00:05:00,090 --> 00:05:04,050
This would have helped the pots to retain water, as they weren't glazed.

68
00:05:04,050 --> 00:05:08,766
Now we know that the clay used usually came from local sources,

69
00:05:08,766 --> 00:05:12,697
which suggests that manufacture was on a fairly small scale,

70
00:05:12,697 --> 00:05:17,020
even though thousands of fragments are usually found at Neolithic sites.

71
00:05:17,020 --> 00:05:21,120
In the course of time decoration began to appear.

72
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:24,060
At first this looked like a series of stitches

73
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:26,400
and was just around the tops of the pots.

74
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:29,480
This could have been an imitation of earlier vessels

75
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:31,800
which were made of leather sewn onto wood.

76
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:34,400
Then eventually pots with decoration all over...
